    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-124201",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a62e8412-1733-577c-bd60-25395e6fa908",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Areas Bounded by Other Polar Curves",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066449/assets/34f6562d5a661d3a58755622571f50752aefa11c8e58b85dad5c70b13c179746.png)\n\nFind the area bounded by the polar curve $r={e}^{\\theta +1}$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ as shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\nWhen $r(\\theta )={e}^{\\theta +1}$, ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ and ${\\theta }_{2}=\\pi $, this gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{({e}^{\\theta +1})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2\\theta +2}{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2\\pi +2}-{e}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2}({e}^{2\\pi }-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
