  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066507",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-86791",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d19a514c-faa5-5f69-9870-87f78878f08a",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Asymptotes of a Vertical Hyperbola by Completing the Square",
      "problem": "Calculate the asymptotes of the hyperbola $16{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+4x+64y+44=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "To rewrite the equation of the hyperbola in standard form, we need to group the $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+4x+64y+44 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{2}+64y-{x}^{2}+4x & =-44 \\\\ 16[{y}^{2}+4y]-[{x}^{2}-4x] & =-44 \\\\ 16[(y+2)^{2}-4]-[(x-2)^{2}-4] & =-44 \\\\ 16(y+2)^{2}-64-(x-2)^{2}+4 & =-44 \\\\ 16(y+2)^{2}-(x-2)^{2} & =-44+64-4 \\\\ 16(y+2)^{2}-(x-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ \\frac{16(y+2)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{16}{16} \\\\ (y+2)^{2}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola\n\n$$\n\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\ny-k=\\pm \\frac{a}{b}(x-h).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n(h,k)=(2,-2),\\,a=\\sqrt{1}=1,\\,b=\\sqrt{16}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the asymptotes of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
