  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066605",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-107795",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7a64d73a-2983-5d05-9aa0-4582b2e7aee1",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Quartic Polynomial Given Four Real Roots",
      "problem": "The quartic polynomial $P(x)$ has roots ${x}_{1}=1,{x}_{2}=2,{x}_{3}=-4$ and ${x}_{4}=4$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $1.$ Find $P(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "Since our quartic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=1,{x}_{2}=2,$ ${x}_{3}=-4,$ and ${x}_{4}=4,$ and the coefficient of the quartic term is $a=1,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3})(x-{x}_{4}) & =1(x-1)(x-2)(x-(-4))(x-4) \\\\  & =(x-1)(x-2)(x+4)(x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x-2)(x+4)(x-4) & =({x}^{2}-3x+2)({x}^{2}-16) \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-16{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3}+48x+2{x}^{2}-32 \\\\  & ={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2}+48x-32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}-14{x}^{2}+48x-32.$",
